      "## Definition\n\nLet $T>0$, let $f\\colon\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous, and let\n$u_0\\in L^\\infty(\\mathbb R)\\cap L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\mathbb R)$. A measurable\nfunction $u\\colon\\mathbb R\\times(0,T)\\to\\mathbb R$ is a **locally bounded\ndistributional weak solution** of $u_t+f(u)_x=0$ with initial datum $u_0$ if\nfor every bounded interval $I\\subset\\mathbb R$ and every $T'<T$ it is\nessentially bounded on $I\\times(0,T')$, and, for every\n$\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R\\times(-\\infty,T))$,\n$$ \\int_0^T\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\bigl(u\\varphi_t+f(u)\\varphi_x\\bigr)\\,dx\\,dt +\\int_{\\mathbb R}u_0(x)\\varphi(x,0)\\,dx=0. $$\nThe integrals are local: if a test is supported in $I\\times[-S,T']$ with $I$\nbounded and $T'<T$, the local essential bound makes $u$ bounded on\n$I\\times(0,T']$, and continuity of $f$ makes $f(u)$ bounded on that bounded\nrange. The initial integral is finite because $u_0$ is locally integrable and\n$\\varphi(\\cdot,0)$ is compactly supported. This is the local\nintegrability version of the weak identity in\n[[def-distributional-weak-solution-of-a-scalar-conservation-law]], whose\npublished formulation assumes a global $L^\\infty$ bound. This definition adds\nno entropy inequality or strong local $L^1$ trace requirement."
    ],
    "def-conservative-finite-volume-scheme": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $T>0$, let $f\\colon\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous, and let\n$u_0\\in L^\\infty(\\mathbb R)\\cap L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\mathbb R)$ be initial data for\n$u_t+f(u)_x=0$ in the convention of\n[[def-scalar-conservation-law-and-flux]]. For each $m\\in\\mathbb N$, choose\n$h_m>0$ and $\\tau_m>0$ with $h_m\\to0$ and $\\tau_m\\to0$. Define the uniform\ncells $I_j^m=[jh_m,(j+1)h_m)$ for $j\\in\\mathbb Z$ and the time levels\n$t_n^m=n\\tau_m$ for $n\\in\\mathbb N_0$.\n\nA continuous **consistent numerical flux** is a continuous map\n$F\\colon\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$ such that $F(a,a)=f(a)$ for every\n$a\\in\\mathbb R$. The associated conservative two-point finite-volume scheme\nstarts from the cell averages\n$$ U_j^{m,0}=\\frac1{h_m}\\int_{I_j^m}u_0(x)\\,dx $$ and uses one shared interface flux $Q_{j+1/2}^{m,n}=F(U_j^{m,n},U_{j+1}^{m,n})$ at every face. Its explicit update is $$ U_j^{m,n+1}=U_j^{m,n}-\\frac{\\tau_m}{h_m}\\left(Q_{j+1/2}^{m,n}-Q_{j-1/2}^{m,n}\\right), \\qquad j\\in\\mathbb Z,\\quad n\\in\\mathbb N_0. $$\nEach internal face flux enters the two adjacent cell updates with opposite\nsigns; this shared-flux difference is the meaning of conservative here. This\ndefinition imposes no CFL condition and no relation between $\\tau_m$ and\n$h_m$.\n\nThe indexing convention is shown below. Each rectangle is one spatial cell;\nthe arrows indicate the signed numerical flux, with positive sign oriented to\nthe right. The same $Q_{j+1/2}^{m,n}$ leaves cell $j$ and enters cell $j+1$.\n\n```tikz\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1.05cm,y=1cm]\n  \\draw (0,0) rectangle (2,0.7);\n  \\draw (2,0) rectangle (4,0.7);\n  \\draw (4,0) rectangle (6,0.7);\n  \\node at (1,0.35) {$U_{j-1}^{m,n}$};\n  \\node at (3,0.35) {$U_j^{m,n}$};\n  \\node at (5,0.35) {$U_{j+1}^{m,n}$};\n  \\draw[->] (1.55,1.15) -- (2.45,1.15);\n  \\draw[->] (3.55,1.15) -- (4.45,1.15);\n  \\draw[->] (5.55,1.15) -- (6.45,1.15);\n  \\node at (2,1.42) {$Q_{j-1/2}^{m,n}$};\n  \\node at (4,1.42) {$Q_{j+1/2}^{m,n}$};\n  \\node at (6,1.42) {$Q_{j+3/2}^{m,n}$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n```"
    ],
    "def-finite-volume-space-time-reconstruction": [
      "## Definition\n\nUse the cells, time levels, states and interface fluxes from\n[[def-conservative-finite-volume-scheme]]. For $J_n^m=[t_n^m,t_{n+1}^m)$,\nthe **piecewise-constant state reconstruction** and **face-flux reconstruction**\nare the measurable functions\n$$ \\mathcal U_m(x,t)=U_j^{m,n},\\qquad \\mathcal Q_m(x,t)=Q_{j+1/2}^{m,n} $$ whenever $(x,t)\\in I_j^m\\times J_n^m$. Values on cell or time-grid boundaries may be assigned arbitrarily, since those boundaries have two-dimensional Lebesgue measure zero. In particular, for almost every $(x,t)$, $$ \\mathcal Q_m(x,t)=F\\!\\left(\\mathcal U_m(x,t),\\mathcal U_m(x+h_m,t)\\right). $$"
    ],
    "def-godunov-flux-for-burgers": [
      "## Definition\n\nFor Burgers' flux $f_B(s)=s^2/2$, define $G\\colon\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$ as follows. If $a\\le b$, set\n$$ G(a,b)=\\begin{cases} a^2/2,&a\\ge0,\\\\ b^2/2,&a<0\\text{ and }b\\le0,\\\\ 0,&a<0<b. \\end{cases} $$ If $b<a$, set $$ G(a,b)=\\frac12\\max\\{a^2,b^2\\}. $$\nThis is the Godunov flux for the convex Burgers flux: for increasing states it\nuses the minimum of $f_B$ on $[a,b]$, and for decreasing states it uses the\nmaximum of $f_B$ on $[b,a]$. It is proposed as a particular numerical flux in\nthe scheme of [[def-conservative-finite-volume-scheme]]. Its continuity and\nconsistency are verified in the pair."
    ],
    "lem-godunov-flux-for-burgers-is-continuous-and-consistent": [
      "## Statement\n\nThe map $G$ defined in [[def-godunov-flux-for-burgers]] is continuous on\n$\\mathbb R^2$ and satisfies $G(a,a)=f_B(a)=a^2/2$ for every $a\\in\\mathbb R$."
    ],
    "lem-finite-volume-discrete-summation-by-parts": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $(U_j^{m,n})$ be a scheme from\n[[def-conservative-finite-volume-scheme]], with reconstruction\n$(\\mathcal U_m,\\mathcal Q_m)$ as in\n[[def-finite-volume-space-time-reconstruction]]. For a test function\n$\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R\\times(-\\infty,T))$, define its space-time\ncell average\n$$ \\Phi_j^{m,n}=\\frac1{h_m\\tau_m}\\int_{t_n^m}^{t_{n+1}^m}\\int_{I_j^m}\\varphi(x,t)\\,dx\\,dt. $$ If $N\\ge1$ is such that $\\varphi$ vanishes for $t\\ge t_{N-1}^m$, then $$ \\sum_{j\\in\\mathbb Z}h_m U_j^{m,0}\\Phi_j^{m,0} +\\sum_{n=1}^{N-1}\\sum_{j\\in\\mathbb Z}h_m U_j^{m,n} (\\Phi_j^{m,n}-\\Phi_j^{m,n-1}) +\\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}\\sum_{j\\in\\mathbb Z}\\tau_m Q_{j+1/2}^{m,n} (\\Phi_{j+1}^{m,n}-\\Phi_j^{m,n})=0. $$\nAll sums are finite because $\\varphi$ is compactly supported in space and time."
    ],
    "lem-conservative-fv-face-fluxes-converge-in-l1loc": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $(\\mathcal U_m,\n\\mathcal Q_m)$ be reconstructions from\n[[def-finite-volume-space-time-reconstruction]] for schemes using a continuous\nconsistent flux $F$ for a continuous physical flux $f$ as in\n[[def-conservative-finite-volume-scheme]]. Suppose $h_m\\to0$,\n$\\mathcal U_m\\to u$ strongly in $L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\mathbb R\\times(0,T))$, and\nfor every bounded interval $I\\subset\\mathbb R$ and $T'<T$ there is\n$M_{I,T'}<\\infty$ such that $|\\mathcal U_m|\\le M_{I,T'}$ almost everywhere on\n$I\\times[0,T']$ for every $m$. Then $u$ is locally essentially bounded up to\nthe initial face and, for every bounded interval $I$ and $T'<T$,\n$$ \\|\\mathcal Q_m-f(u)\\|_{L^1(I\\times(0,T'))}\\longrightarrow0. $$"
    ],
    "thm-conservative-finite-volume-weak-limit": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $T>0$, $f\\colon\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous, $u_0\\in L^\\infty(\\mathbb R)\\cap L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\mathbb R)$, and $F\\colon\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous with $F(a,a)=f(a)$ for every $a\\in\\mathbb R$. For each $m$, form the conservative finite-volume scheme and space-time reconstruction of [[def-conservative-finite-volume-scheme]] and [[def-finite-volume-space-time-reconstruction]] using $h_m,\\tau_m\\to0$ and initial cell averages of $u_0$. Suppose reconstructions converge strongly in $L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\mathbb R\\times(0,T))$ to $u$, and for every bounded interval $I\\subset\\mathbb R$ and $T'<T$ there is $M_{I,T'}<\\infty$ such that $|\\mathcal U_m|\\le M_{I,T'}$ almost everywhere on $I\\times[0,T']$ for every $m$. Then $u$ is a locally bounded distributional weak solution in the sense of [[def-locally-bounded-distributional-weak-solution]]; explicitly, for every $\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R\\times(-\\infty,T))$,\n$$\n\\int_0^T\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\bigl(u\\varphi_t+f(u)\\varphi_x\\bigr)\\,dx\\,dt +\\int_{\\mathbb R}u_0(x)\\varphi(x,0)\\,dx=0.\n$$\nThe strong convergence and local uniform bounds are hypotheses, not conclusions. No CFL condition or entropy inequality is assumed, so the theorem proves a weak limit only; it does not claim Kruzhkov entropy admissibility. If the limit is also globally essentially bounded, this identity is the published bounded weak formulation in [[def-distributional-weak-solution-of-a-scalar-conservation-law]]."
    ],
    "ex-godunov-fv-captures-burgers-shock-speed": [],
